.TH io_uring_buf_ring_head 3 "November 11, 2026" "liburing-2.6" "liburing Manual"
.SH NAME
io_uring_buf_ring_head \- return the kernel head of a provided buffer ring
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <liburing.h>
.PP
.BI "int io_uring_buf_ring_head(struct io_uring *" ring ",
.BI "                           int " buf_group ",
.BI "                           uint16_t *" head ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The
.BR io_uring_buf_ring_head (3)
function queries the kernel for the current head of the provided buffer ring
identified by the buffer group ID
.I buf_group
in the io_uring
.IR ring ,
and stores it in
.IR head .

The application owns the tail of a provided buffer ring and advances it when
adding buffers. The kernel owns the head and advances it as buffers are
consumed, but the head is not part of the memory shared with the application,
hence the need to ask the kernel for it. The buffers between the head and the
tail are the ones that the kernel has not consumed yet, see also
.BR io_uring_buf_ring_available (3).

Available since kernel 6.8.
.SH NOTES
The returned head reflects the state of the buffer ring at the time that it
was queried. If inflight IO exists that may consume provided buffers from this
buffer group, then the returned value is inherently racy.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns 0 on success, with
.I head
filled in. On failure,
.I head
is not modified and a negative errno value is returned:
.TP
.B -ENOENT
The buffer group
.I buf_group
does not exist.
.TP
.B -EINVAL
The buffer group was not registered as a provided buffer ring, or the kernel
does not support this operation.
.TP
.B -EFAULT
The kernel could not copy the status to or from the application.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR io_uring_register_buf_ring (3),
.BR io_uring_setup_buf_ring (3),
.BR io_uring_buf_ring_available (3),
.BR io_uring_buf_ring_advance (3),
.BR io_uring_register (2)
